Author: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date:   Mon Aug 24 10:58:07 2026 +0000

    [REQ-341] Give the browser probe lane trusted input
    
    The lane ran every probe under --headless --dump-dom and read one JSON node
    out of the serialized DOM. That is one crossing, in one direction: enough to
    measure, useless to interact. Four REQs paid for it — REQ-324's lock-in passed
    through the entire click regression, REQ-333 could not drive a captured drag,
    REQ-336's RED had to be reproduced outside the suite, and REQ-337's check had
    to be structural.
    
    Adds a second transport beside the first rather than replacing it:
    --remote-debugging-pipe carries the DevTools Protocol over a pair of inherited
    file descriptors as NUL-terminated JSON, which os/exec and encoding/json
    already cover. No new module dependency; a WebSocket client would have cost
    one. Input.dispatchMouseEvent delivers TRUSTED events, so the engine issues
    the pointerId and setPointerCapture on it establishes a real capture that
    really retargets the synthesized click.
    
    Every measurement on the new transport is wrapped so it carries location.href
    and is checked against the probe page, making the prime's render-evidence rule
    mechanical rather than remembered. Readiness is a polled condition with a
    deadline, never a fixed sleep. Every protocol exchange is bounded by a read
    deadline and every failure is a t.Fatalf carrying the engine's stderr, so a
    transport failure can never read as a probe that measured nothing.
    
    Converts REQ-337's structural check into
    TestBrowserBehaviorTimelinePointerCaptureWaitsForThePanEngage. Four trials on
    one page: a trusted click on a bar opens its drawer; the same click with
    capture taken on pointerdown opens nothing; a trusted drag released above the
    chart ends the pan; and with the host's setPointerCapture swallowed that same
    drag strands it. Each pair proves the other can fail. The structural version's
    recorded residual is closed — the gestures do not care how a capture is
    requested, only whether one exists when the click is synthesized.
    
    The engine-missing SKIP path is unchanged (the new transport resolves its
    browser through lookupBrowserForBehaviorProbe), and it counts itself in
    browserBehaviorProbeCount, so the strict lane's refusal to skip still covers
    it. The renamed test now runs inside the strict lane, which it did not before.
    
    Verified: maintainer-verify.sh exit 0 with a browser supplied; the probe fails
    when REQ-336's defect is reintroduced in board-timeline.js, including when the
    capture is routed through a local variable, which the structural check passed.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
